      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the tentsuyu: simmer dashi, soy sauce, and mirin together for 3 minutes. Keep warm on a low heat."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oil in a Dutch oven to 180°C. Pat the prawns and vegetables completely dry with kitchen paper."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the batter: whisk the egg yolk into the ice-cold water. Add the flour and cornflour all at once and stir briefly with chopsticks or a fork. Leave lumps. Do not overmix."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Dip the prawns in batter one at a time, letting excess drip off, and lower into the oil. Fry 2 to 3 at a time for 2 to 3 minutes until pale golden and crisp. Drain on a wire rack."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Repeat with the vegetables, frying in small batches. Serve immediately on paper with the tentsuyu sauce in small bowls on the side."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Batter is thick and doughy",
              "cause": "The batter was overmixed or the water was not cold enough.",
              "fix": "Mix batter with just 4 to 5 strokes and add a few ice cubes to the water before mixing."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Tempura is greasy",
              "cause": "The oil temperature dropped when too many pieces were added at once.",
              "fix": "Fry in smaller batches and allow the oil to return to 180°C between each batch."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Batter falls off the vegetables",
              "cause": "The vegetables were wet when dipped in batter.",
              "fix": "Pat everything completely dry before battering, and dust lightly with plain flour first."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Tempura entered Japan through Portuguese contact in the sixteenth century. Japanese cooks stripped the technique back and made it lighter. Dedicated tempura restaurants in Tokyo serve it as a formal course with seasonal ingredients. At home, it is a weekend treat that takes a little setup but rewards the effort."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
